Let b and g represent the boy and the girl child, respectively. If a family has two children, the sample space will be S={(b,b),(b,g),(g,b),(g,g)}
Let A be the event that both children are girls. Therefore, A={(g,g)}
Let B be the event that the youngest child is a girl. Therefore, B=[(b,g),(g,g)] ⇒A∩B={(g,g)}
∴P(B)=2/4=1/2 and P(A∩B)=1/4
The conditional probability that both are girls, given that the youngest child is a girl, is given by P(A/B)
P(A/B)=(P(A∩B))/(P(B))=(1/4)/(1/2)=1/2
Let C be the event that at least one child is a girl. Therefore, C={(b,g),(g,b),(g,g)}
⇒A∩C={g,g} ⇒P(C)=3/4 and
P(A∩C)=1/4
The conditional probability that both are girls, given that at least one child is a girl, is given by P(A∣C). Therefore, P(A∣C)=(P(A∩C))/(P(C))=(1/4)/(3/4)=1/3.
